- op: discuss  # c445
  at: '2026-07-29T19:17:52Z'  # c445
  by: agent  # c445
  actor: jgstern_agent  # c445
  clock: 6  # c445
  nonce: c445  # c445
  message: "DONE in 43e71f6031. The always-on ADR-0036 Ruling-1 fold landed at make_symbol_id, and js_ts.py's private f-string copy (the mirror this item predicted) now delegates.\n\nBEHAVIORAL EVIDENCE. The repair is demonstrated by the objc probe, not by the self-corpus: Symbol.name 'FileManager.removeItemAtPath:error:' emitted a SEVEN-segment unparseable id before and a canonical five-segment one after. Stated honestly, the self-corpus does NOT exercise the new behaviour -- all 7 colon-bearing Symbol.names on this repo already routed through sanitize_id_name_segment explicitly, so their ids are byte-identical. Corpus evidence is therefore no-regression only: fresh self-analysis over 40857 symbols holds id_format at 0, and 11742 tests pass at 100% coverage on changed sources. The beneficiary corpora are those with Objective-C / Swift selectors or Maven coordinates.\n\nThe guard test was INVERTED, not deleted, and carries the before/after in its docstring so the reversal is self-documenting rather than looking like someone quietly dropped a check.\n\nPART 4 NOT BUILT -- measured false premise, recorded rather than silently skipped. The description also asks for 'a sibling validator sub-check that the id name-slot == sanitized(Symbol.name)'. That equality is not an invariant this codebase holds: it fails on 1430 of 40855 canonical ids (3.50%). 1398 are the documented make_file_id sentinel {lang}:{path}:1-1:file:file; the residual 32 are four further deliberate conventions -- Rust's '::' -> '.' COLLAPSE (not a per-colon replace, so a naive sanitize comparison mismatches), the js/ts '@NN' occurrence suffix, the synthetic websocket route normalization, and the Maven coordinate fold where the id folds groupId:artifactId while Symbol.name is the artifactId alone. Building it as written would have created a 1430-entry advisory surface that is 98% sentinel.\n\nThe weaker requirement the grammar ACTUALLY states -- name slot colon-free -- is already enforced upstream: _CANONICAL_ID_PATTERN defines the slot as [^:]*, so a colon-bearing name fails the shape check and _check_id_format catches it (reporting colon_in_name_slot since e32d074d2c). _check_id_roundtrip only runs on ids that already matched the pattern, so the requested sub-check would be structurally unreachable code.\n\nIF the enumerated-exception contract is wanted (a real name-slot round-trip with the five conventions declared and gated), that is a design decision deserving its own item -- it would need each exception ratified rather than grandfathered. Not filed unilaterally; flagged here."  # c445
